<config toc toc-location=right toc-depth=2 />

Enscribe Shorthand is the register you write by hand — an ergonomic authoring syntax that keeps the
source close to what you mean. It is one of enscribe's two registers, and everything you write in it
**expands to Layer 1**, the semantic-HTML representation introduced in the
<a href="layer1.html" | Layer 1 intro>. This page introduces the idea and shows a few constructs live;
the comprehensive set lives in the <a href="shorthand-catalog.html" | Shorthand catalog>.

<# #sec:three-forms | One construct, up to three forms #>

Most constructs can be written up to three ways, and all of them compile to the same Layer 1 HTML:

- a **canonical** named tag — `<section | Introduction>`
- a **sigil** shorthand — `<# Introduction #>`
- a **markdown** idiom — `# Introduction`

So a section heading can be authored any of these three ways:

```
<section | Introduction>      (canonical named tag)
<# Introduction #>            (sigil shorthand)
# Introduction                (markdown idiom)
```

Where three forms exist they are interchangeable; where a construct has only one form, the
<a href="shorthand-catalog.html" | Shorthand catalog> says so. Enscribe also delegates to existing
parsers wherever it can — pipe tables, fenced code blocks, emphasis, and other markdown idioms are
accepted as shortcuts — so familiar markup just works.

<# #sec:examples | A few constructs #>

Each example below is pulled live from the vocabulary source — the same source the
<a href="shorthand-catalog.html" | Shorthand catalog> is generated from — so it cannot drift from what
the engine actually accepts. Each shows the authored shorthand, then its rendered result.

<sub-section #featured-section | `<section>`>

<code>
<section | Introduction>
The paper begins here.
</code>

<frame -numbered>
<section | Introduction>
The paper begins here.
</frame>

<sub-section #featured-sub-section | `<sub-section>`>

<code>
<sub-section | Quantitative analysis>
Sub-section content.
</code>

<frame -numbered>
<sub-section | Quantitative analysis>
Sub-section content.
</frame>

<sub-section #featured-fig | `<fig>`>

<code>
<fig src=elephant.jpg | An adult African elephant.>
</code>

<frame -numbered>
<fig -numbered src=elephant.jpg | An adult African elephant.>
</frame>

<sub-section #featured-note | `<note>`>

<code>
Some text<note | A substantive note about the text.>.
</code>

<frame -numbered>
Some text<note | A substantive note about the text.>.
</frame>

<sub-section #featured-inline-math | `<inline-math>`>

<code>
The identity <$ a^2 + b^2 = c^2 $> holds for right triangles.
</code>

<frame -numbered>
The identity <$ a^2 + b^2 = c^2 $> holds for right triangles.
</frame>

<sub-section #featured-abbr | `<abbr>`>

<code>
The <abbr title="Document Object Model" | DOM> is the browser API for HTML.
</code>

<frame -numbered>
The <abbr title="Document Object Model" | DOM> is the browser API for HTML.
</frame>

<sub-section #featured-term | `<term>`>

<code>
An <term | eigenvector> is a non-zero vector that scales under a linear transformation.
</code>

<frame -numbered>
An <term | eigenvector> is a non-zero vector that scales under a linear transformation.
</frame>

<sub-section #featured-theorem | `<theorem>`>

<code>
<theorem | If $a^2 + b^2 = c^2$ then the triangle is right-angled.>
</code>

<frame -numbered>
<theorem -numbered | If $a^2 + b^2 = c^2$ then the triangle is right-angled.>
</frame>


